FAIL BASIS (one defect, criterion (b), steps 4 and 7): the cleanup does not survive further PROCESS-GROUP signals. It leaks the run's compose network.
No secret, no unchecked file, no live Codex was found anywhere (every run below: leak scan of audit dir + verdicts = clean).

F1. Group SIGINT/SIGTERM during cleanup kills the docker children of teardown(); the compose project is left behind.
  Contract: step 4 "torn down on every exit path (containers, networks, volumes)"; step 7 "INT, TERM and HUP each lead into cleanup; once cleanup starts it ignores
  them"; the brief names SIGKILL as the ONLY case that may leave the project ("A SIGKILL (untrappable) can leave behind the private temp dir and the run's compose
  project"); attempt-3 reopen scope item (4) "cleanup ignores further signals" (SPEC ruling CD-n); the lead's own oracle invariant "H no networks left behind by any
  trappable run".
  Mechanism (measured): cleanup() does trap '' INT TERM HUP, so bash and Python children inherit SIG_IGN (the 264-run fake sweep and Python keep survive), but the Go docker CLI reinstalls INT and TERM handlers.
     bash -c 'trap "" TERM INT HUP; exec docker events' -> /proc/PID/status SigIgn=0000000000000001 (HUP only); kill -TERM and kill -INT kill it, kill -HUP does not.
     A terminal Ctrl-C (and coreutils timeout without --foreground) delivers to the whole process group, i.e. to whichever docker child is running (known behaviour; I emulated it with kill -SIG -- -PGID).
  Repro (real docker, harness started with setsid so kill -SIG -- -PGID is a terminal-style group signal; trigger = the run's own "docker compose -p <project> ... down"
     process appears; scenario = stub plants a fake token file + event, writes a valid last.json, exits; measured docker network ls before/after):
     3 x group TERM, 0.4 s apart:    g03, h1, h2, h3, h4, p1, p2      -> 7/7 runs leave one <project>_isolated network (containers unchanged, verdict written, rc=0)
     3 x group INT:                  p3                               -> leaves one network
     2 x group TERM, 0.4 s apart:    p4                               -> leaves one network
     2 x group INT,  0.4 s apart:    d1                               -> leaves one network
     INT then 5 group INTs (s2), 8 group INTs (s4), both started mid-run:       -> leave one network each
     NOT leaking (controls): ONE group INT/TERM on compose down (g01, d4, d5); two group INTs 1.0 s / 2.0 s apart (d2, d3); a single-PID TERM during compose down (c01) and
     during tools.py keep (c03) (rc=0, verdict, 0 leftovers); plain runs (ev, net, aw); barrages whose signals landed on other steps (m01-m03 = 10 group INT/TERM, s1, s3).
     (c02 and g04 aimed at compose logs never saw the trigger and are not evidence.) No run left a container or a live Codex (LIVE_CHECKER=0 in every run that measured it).
  Remedy verified against the defect (rule 6b): in my copy t2 the six docker invocations of stop_container()/teardown() run under "setsid -w" (docker rm -f -v,
     compose logs, compose down, docker ps -aq, docker rm, docker network ls/rm). Same barrage, same trigger: q1 (3 x gTERM), q2 (3 x gTERM), q3 (3 x gINT), q4 (2 x gTERM)
     -> 0 leftover networks (agents2-codex-* count 8 -> 8), verdict written, rc=0. The fix is inside CD1 territory (swarm/codex-check.sh), a few lines.
  Why the oracle passes: its TERM/INT/HUP cases signal the harness PID only (kill PID), never the process group; H-checks compare counts after those runs.
  Severity, stated plainly: an empty docker network (each holds a subnet from Docker's address pools). Needs
  >=2 group signals within ~1 s during the ~1-2 s compose down window (a fast double Ctrl-C). A judge may weigh that low; it is real, reproduced in all 12 fast multi-signal runs, and covered by the written text. Whether repeated leaks exhaust Docker's address pools was not tested.

