OBSERVATIONS (not grounds for FAIL)
  O1. Recognised-forms net only: a name that is the first 60 chars of the JWT was kept as out/<name> (partial secret), a secret split across two verdict fields was written to the verdict, and base64/hex/split/upper-cased
      copies are kept. All are outside the brief's forms; a deliberately encoding Codex is not stopped, only accidental disclosure is.
  O2. tools.py is fed the secret list as env CD_SECRETS=<JSON list of every auth string> through the external command env, so the whole list is in argv of three short-lived env processes (copy, keep, verdict):
      strace -f -e trace=execve shows execve("/usr/bin/env", ["env", "CD_SECRETS=[\"...\", ...]", ...]); readable via /proc/PID/cmdline for ~1 ms (my poller never caught the window). Not a file; the brief says "environment or pipes".
      A bash prefix assignment (CD_SECRETS=... python3 ...) would keep it out of argv.
  O3. bash 5.2 here-strings use a pipe up to 64 KiB but an unlinked temp file in $TMPDIR above it (strace: sh-thd.*): a secret JSON over 64 KiB (never a real auth.json) would touch disk in the IFS read; over 128 KiB the exec fails E2BIG and every step skips (fail-closed).
  O4. dropped.txt is unbounded (39,939 lines for 40,000 planted entries; names are capped, count is not); an events.jsonl over 16 MiB is dropped wholesale.
  O5. The noline DETAIL echoes a manifest path even when absolute: printf 'src/a.txt\n/etc/shadow\n' as manifest without a sidecar line -> "skip fingerprint-mismatch noline /etc/shadow" (the regex allows a leading /); manifest text, not a harness-derived host path.
  O6. Duplicate JSON keys: {"tokens":{"access_token":"<S>","access_token":"short"}} -> json.loads keeps the last, the first value is not in the secret set (with no other string: skip nosecret; with another secret present it would not be covered).
  O7. Exclusion is by name: a hard link of data/live.csv named src/hardlink.txt is copied with its content (attempt-1 O2, unchanged); container mountinfo still shows host source paths (user name, temp dirs) (attempt-2 observation).
  O8. First-batch caveat: my earliest signal batch (b01-b14) used a pgrep trigger that matched my own command line, so it fired at t=0; I discarded it and redid everything with a session-scoped/project-scoped trigger. Numbers above are from the redone runs.
